    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass. There's a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`) with 2 tests, plus the full build (which includes OpenAPI DTO generation and MapStruct mapper compilation) succeeded \u2014 confirming the generated `OwnerDto.deleted` field maps cleanly from the entity.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented owner soft-delete across the stack:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean column (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`), initialized to `false`, with getter/setter. A newly created owner is therefore `false`.\n\n2. **OpenAPI spec** (`resources/openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a `deleted` (readOnly boolean) property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it onto the GET response.\n\n3. **Database schemas** \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` to the `owners` table in all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, plus h2, mysql, postgres). The `DEFAULT FALSE` keeps seed rows valid.\n\n4. **Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n   - `deleteOwner` now marks the owner `deleted = true` and saves it (retaining the row) instead of hard-deleting, returning `204` (2xx). GET afterward still returns the owner with `deleted: true`.\n   - `rejectDuplicate` now skips owners flagged `deleted`, so a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only matching owner has been soft-deleted. Updated the javadoc accordingly.\n\nBoth acceptance tests \u2014 `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` and `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck` \u2014 pass, and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nI scoped the \"ignore deleted\" behavior to the duplicate/identity check exactly as the specification states, leaving the other count-based rules (city capacity, daily limit, namesake, etc.) unchanged.",
